+## What Coding Harness adds
+
+These use harness capabilities other hosts lack. Each is on by default where it
+costs nothing, and a setting under `harness` in `aidlc.settings.json` (project,
+local, or machine layer) tunes it.
+
+### Cross-vendor review
+
+A reviewer (`aidlc-architecture-reviewer-agent`, `aidlc-product-lead-agent`,
+`aidlc-compliance-agent`, and any plugin `*-reviewer-agent`) is spawned on a
+model family other than the one doing the work, so it does not share the
+builder's blind spots. The adapter tries, in order, the reviewer's own models,
+`harness.reviewPool`, and the `@slow` and `@default` roles, and leads with the
+first that resolves to another family; the reviewer's own models stay behind it
+as fallbacks. When no configured model belongs to another family, nothing
+changes.
+
+```json
+{
+  "schemaVersion": 1,
+  "harness": { "schemaVersion": 1, "reviewPool": ["openai/gpt-5.5"] }
+}
+```
+
+Set `"reviewDiversity": "off"` to keep reviewers on the builder's model. To keep
+code inside a boundary (for example local models only for a client repository),
+disable the other providers for that path with Coding Harness's path-scoped
+`disabledProviders`; a reviewer never resolves to a disabled provider.
+
 ## Alongside Claude Code
 
 One project can carry both distributions: run `aidlc config --harness claude`
